      "destinationText": "03Search by layersHow can the first encounter with the goal prove the fewest moves? Breadth-first search (BFS) explores one move count at a time. The frontier contains every newly discovered state at distance d; seen is the set of all discovered states. The start is layer 0 because no move has been made.Keep 123405: 0 occupies position 4 (zero-based, row-major), so it can swap only with positions 1, 3, 5. This gives 103425, 123045, 123450, each reachable in 1 move. The last is the goal and layer 0 was not, so the answer is 1. Later, when 103425 generates 123405, seen rejects it: that board was already found at layer 0, and returning cannot improve its distance.This yields the invariant: on entering layer d, the frontier is exactly the states whose shortest distance is d, and seen is exactly those within distance d. One edge from the frontier reaches a state within d+1 moves. An unseen state cannot be within d moves, or it would already have been discovered. Its distance is therefore exactly d+1. Mark it seen immediately to avoid duplicates within the new layer. Every (d+1)-move path has a penultimate state within d moves, so the next layer misses no state either. This proof relies on every edge costing one move.Word Ladder II: retain shortest paths by layer →Finish “One move”, then select “Five moves”. Advance layer by layer and inspect the retained layer ledger. Each frame sho",
